  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1234 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nSunday Night-Tuesday...A shortwave trough over the Desert \nSouthwest ejects to the northeast into the Northern Plains. A \nFront Range low across eastern Colorado is expected to deepen \nand track across the Central Plains into Minnesota Monday into \nTuesday. This passage will wash out the weak front draped across\nIowa and northern Illinois as winds shift from easterly back to\nsoutherly. Mid-level moisture advection could be sufficient \nenough to support isolated rain showers (10-20% chance) Monday\nnight into Tuesday.\n\nTuesday Night-Wednesday...Another shortwave trough swings into the \nNorthern Plains Tuesday night. Surface cyclogenesis will occur \nquickly in the wake of the previous low pressure, but the fast \npropagation speed of the trough is expected to limit significant \npressure falls. By Wednesday evening, the new low pressure moves \nacross Minnesota into southern Canada, dragging a cold front\nsoutheastward into eastern Iowa and northern Illinois. PoPs on \nWednesday again will be isolated (10-30%) due to the lack of \nlow-level moisture return.\n\nThursday-Friday...In wake of Wednesday's cold front, westerly flow \ncould advect in some cooler air with highs falling back in the \n60s and low 70s, still above average for the end of October. \nThis will not last long though as high pressure over the Ohio \nRiver Valley shifts our winds back out of the south Thursday\nnight, keeping small PoPs (20-40%) around late week.",
